VALUES (460097193102015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “General Organic Chemistry”, how should functional groups be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”, how should Importance of Chemistry be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”, how should d and f orbitals be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”, how should molar mass and mole concept be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** functional groups is listed in the unit “General Organic Chemistry”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to General Organic Chemistry, tetravalency of carbon, hybridization, sp2 and sp3 hybrid orbitals with examples. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “General Organic Chemistry”. Relate it to General Organic Chemistry; tetravalency of carbon; hybridization; sp2 and sp3 hybrid orbitals with examples. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “General Organic Chemistry”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Importance of Chemistry is listed in the unit “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry, Nature of matter, classification based on physical state, classification based on chemical structure. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry; Nature of matter; classification based on physical state; classification based on chemical structure. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** d and f orbitals is listed in the unit “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry, Importance of Chemistry, Nature of matter, classification based on physical state. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry; Importance of Chemistry; Nature of matter; classification based on physical state. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** molar mass and mole concept is listed in the unit “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry, Importance of Chemistry, Nature of matter, classification based on physical state. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Relate it to Basic Concepts of Chemistry; Importance of Chemistry; Nature of matter; classification based on physical state. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

