VALUES (4600971931020129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] In a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task within “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with Importance of Chemistry. Explain how Importance of Chemistry applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task within “General Organic Chemistry”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with tetravalency of carbon. Explain how tetravalency of carbon applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task within “Electromotive Force”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with Types of electrochemical cells and examples. Explain how Types of electrochemical cells and examples applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task within “Polymers”, the requirement is large molecules made of repeating structural units called monomers. Explain how classification of polymers applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Importance of Chemistry is covered in “Basic Concepts of Chemistry”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to Nature of matter, classification based on physical state, classification based on chemical structure, Physical quantities, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to Nature of matter, classification based on physical state, classification based on chemical structure, Physical quantities. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** tetravalency of carbon is covered in “General Organic Chemistry”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to hybridization, sp2 and sp3 hybrid orbitals with examples, functional groups, IUPAC nomenclature of organic compounds, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to hybridization, sp2 and sp3 hybrid orbitals with examples, functional groups, IUPAC nomenclature of organic compounds. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Types of electrochemical cells and examples is covered in “Electromotive Force”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to Half- cell, Reversible and irreversible cell, Convention sign, Types of electrodes, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to Half- cell, Reversible and irreversible cell, Convention sign, Types of electrodes. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Polymers contain repeating units; properties depend on monomer chemistry, chain structure and processing.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to industrial manufacturing, properties and uses of some important polymers: Polythene, Teflon, Polyacrylonitrile. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical APPLIED CHEMISTRY task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- BHARATIYA KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 460099110101015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (460099110101015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply meaning, scope and classification of bharatiya knowledge systems in a practical situation such as organizing a course presentation across language, philosophy, science and technology. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply caturdasa-vidyasthana and the traditional knowledge disciplines in a practical situation such as classifying a Sanskrit grammar lesson within the traditional framework. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply the four vedas and their broad roles in a practical situation such as distinguishing a hymn collection from a collection associated with melodies. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply vedangas and their role in studying vedic texts in a practical situation such as choosing the relevant discipline for pronunciation, metre or grammar. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nBharatiya Knowledge Systems (BKS) refers to knowledge traditions developed and transmitted in the Indian subcontinent across intellectual, practical, cultural and philosophical fields. Depending on the syllabus framework, areas include Vedic and philosophical literature, language sciences, mathematics, astronomy, medicine, arts, engineering, architecture and environmental practices. Classification helps organize sources and disciplines without treating every tradition as identical. A sound explanation names the field, identifies its historical or textual context, and distinguishes documented practices from later interpretation. Claims should be supported by the relevant text, artifact or historical evidence rather than generalized to all people or periods.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nCaturdasa-Vidyasthana is a traditional classification of fourteen knowledge disciplines. A common presentation counts the four Vedas, six Vedangas and four additional knowledge traditions such as Mimamsa, Nyaya, Purana and Dharmashastra, though descriptions can vary by source. The framework shows how textual study, interpretation, language, ritual, reasoning and normative traditions were organized. When explaining it, identify the components, show how the categories relate, and note that the scheme is a traditional taxonomy rather than a modern university department list.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe four Vedas are Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda and Atharvaveda. The Rigveda is known for hymns; the Samaveda is closely associated with chants and melodies; the Yajurveda contains prose formulas and material related to ritual performance; and the Atharvaveda includes hymns and material concerning everyday life and ritual. Their contents overlap and their textual traditions are complex, so descriptions should be broad rather than absolute. Explain each name and its principal association, and avoid attributing every later scientific or social idea directly to all four Vedas.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe six Vedangas are Shiksha (phonetics/pronunciation), Chandas (metre), Vyakarana (grammar), Nirukta (etymology or explanation of words), Kalpa (ritual procedure) and Jyotisha (astronomical/calendrical timing). They support accurate recitation, interpretation and performance connected with Vedic literature. They are complementary: phonetics helps pronunciation, grammar analyzes forms, metre studies poetic structure, and Jyotisha relates to timekeeping. A complete answer names the six fields and explains at least one practical role of each instead of confusing them with modern engineering subjects.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

