-- BHARATIYA KNOWLEDGE SYSTEM | ESE | medium | TestId 4600991101010129 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4600991101010129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply metallurgy and iron/steel technology in historical india in a practical situation such as evaluating an artifact or historical description of iron production. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply traditional textile technology and dyeing in a practical situation such as describing how a natural dye process moves from source material to finished cloth. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply water management and rainwater harvesting in a practical situation such as planning a system for collecting seasonal rainfall in a settlement. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply sacred ecology, forests and agricultural knowledge in a practical situation such as assessing a claim connecting seasonal observations with farm decisions. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nMetallurgy includes obtaining metals from ores and shaping or treating them for use. A historical discussion can cover ore preparation, heating and reduction, forging, joining, heat treatment and the properties sought, while recognizing that techniques varied across periods and regions. Iron and steel production requires evidence about the artifact, process and material; the existence of a notable artifact does not prove that one technique was universal. A well-supported answer connects process to properties such as strength, hardness, corrosion behaviour or workability and identifies the limits of the available evidence.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTextile technology includes fibre preparation, spinning, weaving and finishing. Dyeing adds colour using a dye source and a process suited to the fibre; preparation, temperature, time and mordants can affect shade and colour fastness. Historical Indian textile traditions included sophisticated regional practices, but materials and techniques differed. To analyze a process, identify the fibre, colour source, preparation, application and finishing steps, then explain how these steps influence the result. Avoid treating all traditional dyes or textile methods as chemically or historically identical.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nWater management combines collection, storage, distribution, drainage and maintenance. Rainwater harvesting captures runoff from a roof or catchment, conveys it through channels, filters or settles sediment, and stores or recharges it where appropriate. Traditional systems were adapted to local rainfall, terrain, materials and community needs; Gujarat and other regions contain different examples. A useful analysis checks catchment area, water demand, overflow, contamination prevention and maintenance responsibility. Historical systems should be explained in their local setting rather than assumed to work unchanged in every modern site.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTraditional ecological knowledge may connect land, water, forests, seasonal cycles and social or religious practices. A study of Nakshatra-related agricultural knowledge should identify the particular calendar or observational claim and compare it with the local context and available evidence. Forest management and sacred natural sites can also be studied for their cultural meaning and practical conservation effects. Do not assume that every community followed one identical practice or that cultural association alone proves a measurable ecological outcome. Distinguish historical description, current practice and independently tested environmental effects.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 460009382101015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (460009382101015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Various laboratory equipment and devices”, how should instruments and their practice be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”, how should Covers be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Various laboratory equipment and devices”, how should Various laboratory equipment and devices be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical situation within “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”, how should and Supports be applied? Explain the method, identify the governing conditions and justify the expected result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** instruments and their practice is listed in the unit “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to Various laboratory equipment and devices, Basic chemical engineering symbols, Laboratory glass ware. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. Relate it to Various laboratory equipment and devices; Basic chemical engineering symbols; Laboratory glass ware. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Pressure vessels contain pressurized fluids and require design consideration of pressure, temperature, materials, joints, corrosion and applicable codes.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”. Relate it to Storage vessels and Pressure vessel; Types of storage vessel and pressure vessel in detail; Heads; and Supports. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Various laboratory equipment and devices is listed in the unit “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. Explain its definition and purpose, identify the important variables, equipment or steps specified in the course material, and relate it to Basic chemical engineering symbols, Laboratory glass ware, instruments and their practice. Use a relevant chemical-engineering example, maintain consistent units or operating conditions where applicable, and state how the result should be checked.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. Relate it to Basic chemical engineering symbols; Laboratory glass ware; instruments and their practice. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Pressure vessels contain pressurized fluids and require design consideration of pressure, temperature, materials, joints, corrosion and applicable codes.\n\n**Principles/procedure (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical sequence, including the relevant process steps, variables, equipment or formulas from “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”. Relate it to Storage vessels and Pressure vessel; Types of storage vessel and pressure vessel in detail; Heads; Covers. Where a numerical relationship is required, define the quantities, use consistent units and state the operating assumptions.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply the concept to a chemical-engineering process, laboratory task or equipment-selection problem within “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”. State the expected outcome and why the chosen approach fits.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Compare the result with the required process condition or design objective, and mention a relevant error source, safety consideration or limitation.\n\nMarking guide: meaning/purpose 2; principles/procedure 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1. Confirm technical details against official teaching material."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

