VALUES (4600093821010129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] In a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task within “Various laboratory equipment and devices”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with Basic chemical engineering symbols. Explain how Basic chemical engineering symbols applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task within “Block Diagram and Flow diagram”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with Basic of block diagram and flow diagram of chemical process industries. Explain how Basic of block diagram and flow diagram of chemical process industries applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task within “Storage vessels and Pressure vessel”, the requirement is a closed shell designed to safely contain fluid at specified pressure and temperature. Explain how Types of storage vessel and pressure vessel in detail applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] In a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task within “Reaction Vessel”, the requirement is the function or principle associated with Types of reaction vessel in detail. Explain how Types of reaction vessel in detail applies and justify the method with an example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Basic chemical engineering symbols is covered in “Various laboratory equipment and devices”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to Laboratory glass ware, instruments and their practice, Various laboratory equipment and devices, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to Laboratory glass ware, instruments and their practice, Various laboratory equipment and devices. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Basic of block diagram and flow diagram of chemical process industries is covered in “Block Diagram and Flow diagram”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to Block Diagram and Flow diagram, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to Block Diagram and Flow diagram. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** A pressure vessel contains fluid at design pressure and temperature; design considers allowable stress, joints, corrosion allowance, supports and applicable codes.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to Heads, Covers, and Supports, Storage vessels and Pressure vessel. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2):** Types of reaction vessel in detail is covered in “Reaction Vessel”. Define its purpose, explain the main steps or components, relate it to glass line reactor jacketed reactor etc, and different parts of reactor vessel, Reaction Vessel, give a practical example, and state one check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Explain the concept in sequence and connect it to glass line reactor jacketed reactor etc, and different parts of reactor vessel, Reaction Vessel. State relevant conditions, components or formulas.\n\n**Application (2):** Apply it to a practical CHEMICAL ENGINEERING EQUIPMENT DESIGN task and state the expected outcome.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result against the requirement and state a limitation, risk or error source.\n\nMarking guide: definition 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- COMMUNICATION SKILLS | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 460919120103015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (460919120103015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply skimming, scanning, intensive and extensive reading in a practical situation such as finding a date quickly before reading a technical article in depth. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply reading comprehension and evidence-based answers in a practical situation such as answering an inference question from a passage about a new campus service. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply english tenses and consistency of time in a practical situation such as describing a completed lab visit and a result that is still valid. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply parts of speech and their functions in a practical situation such as editing a sentence where a modifier is attached to the wrong word. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nSkimming is a rapid read to identify the general idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail such as a date or name. Intensive reading examines a short passage carefully for meaning, vocabulary and relationships, while extensive reading builds fluency through longer texts for general understanding. Select the technique to match the purpose, preview headings or key words, and return to the passage for evidence before answering. These methods complement rather than replace one another; a reader often skims first, scans for details and then reads a critical section intensively.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nComprehension requires identifying the central idea, supporting details, sequence, purpose, tone and implied meaning. Direct questions can often be answered from an explicit sentence; inference questions require connecting evidence and background understanding without inventing facts. Read the question carefully, locate relevant lines, distinguish fact from opinion and summarize in one''s own words when appropriate. A correct answer should be supported by the passage, not merely by personal belief. For unfamiliar vocabulary, use surrounding sentences and word structure as clues, then verify the interpretation in context.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTense and aspect locate an action in time and describe whether it is ongoing, completed or connected to another time. Simple forms express facts or events; continuous forms emphasize duration; perfect forms connect an earlier action with a reference time. Maintain consistent time references unless the meaning requires a shift. For example, a report of a completed visit generally uses past forms for events, while a general conclusion may use present tense. Check time expressions and the intended chronology when choosing a verb form.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nNouns name people, places, things or ideas; pronouns replace noun phrases; verbs express actions or states; adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs. Prepositions express relationships, conjunctions connect units, and interjections express reactions. A word''s part of speech depends on how it functions in the sentence, not only on its spelling. During editing, identify the word''s role and check whether modifiers are placed clearly and whether each clause is complete.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

